"""Extra depth for Couture hub articles (added Oct 2026 after the AdSense 'low value content' review).
Blocks are inserted before each article's closing call-to-action / sources. Add only; never remove."""
MORE = {
'couture-news-paris-july-2026': [
 ('h2', 'Reading the week as a designer'),
 ('p', 'Couture weeks are not shopping events; very few people buy from them directly. Their value for the rest of the industry is as a laboratory. Techniques, proportions and finishes that appear here usually reach occasion wear, bridal and premium ready-to-wear one to three seasons later, simplified and made for production. The useful question is therefore not "what was shown" but "which ideas can be translated into my price point".'),
 ('h2', 'Three translations for ready-to-wear'),
 ('ul', ['<b>Hand technique as the focal point.</b> Couture uses embroidery across whole garments. Ready-to-wear can borrow one zone of it: a yoke, a cuff, a neckline or a hem border, worked by hand on an otherwise simple piece. This keeps cost under control while giving the product a story.',
         '<b>Structure without weight.</b> Sculptural shapes in couture are built with boning, tulle and hand-shaped interfacing. The commercial version is a structured bodice, a corset-seamed top or a peplum built with light interfacing and princess seams.',
         '<b>Volume from light fabrics.</b> Hand-pleated chiffon and organza bows translate into pleated organza sleeves, bow details and gathered tulle skirts in eveningwear and festive collections.']),
 ('h2', 'Why the Indian debuts matter beyond India'),
 ('p', 'When Indian couturiers show on the official Paris calendar, techniques such as zardozi, chikankari, resham and gota stop being read only as "ethnic" and become part of the global luxury vocabulary. For international brands this widens the market for embroidery made in Indian clusters; for Indian brands it supports higher price positioning for hand work at home and abroad. Expect more collaborations between European houses and Indian embroidery ateliers, which already supply much of the world\'s luxury embellishment.'),
 ('h2', 'How it reads by region'),
 ('ul', ['<b>Europe and North America:</b> sculptural eveningwear and hand-pleated details for gala, wedding guest and red-carpet categories.',
         '<b>Middle East (GCC):</b> embellished, covered couture silhouettes for Eid and weddings: long sleeves, high necks and fluid layers carry embroidery well.',
         '<b>India and South Asia:</b> lighter bridal pieces that keep heavy craft but reduce weight, a direction Forecut tracks across the wedding seasons.',
         '<b>East Asia:</b> technical craft and innovation stories, such as glass and hand-blown elements, resonate with clients who value precision and novelty.']),
 ('h2', 'What to do next'),
 ('ul', ['Pick one couture technique and design a single focal placement for your next occasion range.', 'Brief your embroidery unit on lighter bases (organza, tulle, georgette) for the same motif.', 'Use process content, showing hands at work and hours spent, to explain price, as couture houses do.']),
],
'couture-india-couture-week-2026': [
 ('h2', 'Why these trends will travel'),
 ('p', 'India Couture Week sets the agenda for the wedding season that follows, from October to February. Bridal and trousseau customers often see the runway looks on social media first, then ask local designers and boutiques for versions in their budget. That makes the week one of the clearest demand signals in Indian occasion wear.'),
 ('h2', 'Turning the trends into product'),
 ('ul', ['<b>Ceremonial headpieces:</b> develop matching headpieces, matha pattis and veil crowns as add-on items. They raise the average order value and are easy to ship.',
         '<b>Sculptural corsets:</b> a corset-structured blouse or bodice gives a lehenga a modern line. Build it with spiral steel or synthetic boning, a firm interlining and a waist stay, and allow at least two fittings.',
         '<b>Jewellery built into garments:</b> place beadwork and crystal clusters where necklaces and belts would sit, so the outfit needs fewer separate pieces.',
         '<b>Bridal red:</b> offer red in several values, from vermilion to deep maroon, and pair it with antique gold rather than bright yellow gold for a current look.',
         '<b>Artisan credit:</b> name the technique, the region and, where the makers agree, the karigars on the product page and swing tag.']),
 ('h2', 'Planning by price point'),
 ('p', 'At couture level, structure and embellishment are built by hand around one client. At premium made-to-order, use set patterns with two or three bodice sizes adjusted at a fitting, and keep hand work to the bodice and dupatta border. At accessible bridal and guest wear, use machine embroidery with hand-finished highlights, and lighter cancan instead of heavy layers.'),
 ('h2', 'The international view'),
 ('p', 'The same ideas suit South Asian diaspora weddings in the UK, North America, the Gulf and Australia, where clients want Indian craft in silhouettes that also work for receptions and Western-style events. Corset bodices, capes and sculptural veils are especially easy to adapt for these markets.'),
 ('h2', 'Timing'),
 ('p', 'Most Indian wedding dates fall between late October and February, with a second window in April and May. Sampling for the runway-inspired pieces should be finished before the main season starts, which means placing embroidery orders in late summer.'),
],
'couture-what-is-haute-couture': [
 ('h2', 'Who decides'),
 ('p', 'The official list of haute couture houses is managed in Paris by the Fédération de la Haute Couture et de la Mode, the body that also runs the Paris fashion weeks. Membership and the guest designers invited to show on the couture calendar are reviewed each season. Because the designation is protected in France, brands elsewhere use words such as couture, alta moda, bespoke or made-to-measure to describe their work.'),
 ('h2', 'What happens inside a couture order'),
 ('ul', ['<b>Consultation:</b> the client chooses a look from the collection or commissions a variation, with fabric and colour changes.',
         '<b>Measurements and toile:</b> a fitting garment in plain cotton is made and adjusted on the client.',
         '<b>Fittings:</b> usually several, as the real garment is built and the embroidery added.',
         '<b>Finishing and delivery:</b> hand-sewn linings, hems and closures, then final pressing and delivery, often with aftercare.']),
 ('h2', 'Couture in Indian fashion'),
 ('p', 'Indian couture is built mainly on bridal and occasion wear, where garments are made around one client and decorated with hand embroidery such as zardozi, aari, resham, gota and chikankari. It follows the wider meaning of couture: made to measure and hand finished, but not the protected French designation. Indian couture weeks organised by the Fashion Design Council of India and other bodies showcase this work.'),
 ('h2', 'How to describe your work honestly'),
 ('ul', ['Say what is hand made and what is machine made.', 'State whether the piece is made to measure, made to order in a standard size, or ready to wear.', 'Give the number of fittings included and the lead time.', 'Name the techniques and, where possible, the region they come from.']),
 ('p', 'Clear language protects the brand. A customer who buys a made-to-order lehenga described accurately is far more likely to return than one who expected couture and received a standard-size piece.'),
 ('h2', 'Why couture still matters to everyone else'),
 ('p', 'Couture is where new techniques and silhouettes are proven. Ready-to-wear, bridal and occasion brands use it as a forecast of craft: the embroidery placements, fabric choices and structures shown in couture often appear, simplified, in commercial collections one to three seasons later.'),
],
'couture-brand-theory': [
 ('h2', '6. Pricing that reflects the work'),
 ('p', 'Couture pricing starts with hours, not fabric. Estimate pattern-making, toiles, fittings, embroidery hours per square metre of work, finishing and overheads, then add the brand premium. Publishing a "from" price for bridal and explaining what drives the final price (embroidery area, technique, fabric and lead time) builds trust and filters enquiries.'),
 ('h2', '7. Protecting the codes'),
 ('ul', ['Document signature motifs and silhouettes with dated sketches and photographs.', 'Register the brand name and key logos as trademarks in each market you sell in.', 'Keep a design archive: past collections become reference for future ones and for heritage storytelling.', 'Use consistent naming for signature techniques so customers learn to ask for them.']),
 ('h2', '8. Choosing where to show'),
 ('p', 'A couture or bridal label can build visibility through a fashion or couture week, private trunk shows for clients in other cities, appointment-only studio presentations, or digital lookbooks. Each has a different cost and audience. Many growing labels start with trunk shows and digital content and add a runway show when the client base can support it.'),
 ('h2', 'The house model in different markets'),
 ('ul', ['<b>Europe:</b> heritage houses fund couture through accessories, beauty and ready-to-wear.',
         '<b>India:</b> bridal couture is itself a strong business; prêt lines and festive collections widen the customer base.',
         '<b>Middle East:</b> eveningwear and bridal houses build loyal private clienteles and seasonal Eid collections.',
         '<b>United States:</b> red-carpet visibility and bridal salons drive the made-to-order business.']),
 ('h2', 'A checklist for a growing label'),
 ('ul', ['Can a customer describe your signature in one sentence?', 'Is your atelier visible in your content?', 'Do you have a clear entry product below bridal?', 'Do you keep client records for sizes, preferences and occasions?', 'Is your pricing explained, not just quoted?']),
],
'couture-storytelling': [
 ('h2', 'Step 6: test the story with a capsule'),
 ('p', 'Before sampling the full line-up, make three looks that represent the opening, middle and finale. Photograph them together under the same light. If the three pieces do not obviously belong to one idea, adjust the palette, technique or silhouette now, when changes are still cheap.'),
 ('h2', 'Step 7: write the collection notes'),
 ('p', 'Every couture collection needs a short written note for press, buyers and clients: the one-sentence idea, the inspiration, the key techniques and the materials. The same text becomes product descriptions, social captions and the script for appointments. Writing it early keeps the team aligned.'),
 ('h2', 'A worked example'),
 ('ul', ['<b>Sentence:</b> "A monsoon garden seen through old palace windows."',
         '<b>Three moods:</b> rain on stone (greys and silver), wet leaves (deep greens), palace interiors (ivory and antique gold).',
         '<b>Palette:</b> slate, moss, bottle green, ivory, antique gold, with a single accent of rain-washed pink.',
         '<b>Materials:</b> organza and tissue for lightness, raw silk for structure, velvet for the finale.',
         '<b>Hero technique:</b> tonal resham leaves; <b>supporting:</b> fine dabka outlines that echo window lattices.',
         '<b>Line-up:</b> light organza looks open; structured raw silk builds the middle; an embroidered velvet cape closes.']),
 ('h2', 'Common mistakes'),
 ('ul', ['Too many techniques: three or four hero techniques compete and the story disappears.', 'A palette chosen from trend reports alone, without a link to the story.', 'Fabric chosen flat on the table, not tested for drape and embroidery weight.', 'No variation in scale: the same motif at the same size on every look feels repetitive.']),
 ('h2', 'Using Forecut in the process'),
 ('p', 'Use Forecut\'s season themes and colour pages as a check, not a starting point. Once your story is set, compare your palette and techniques with what is rising for the season and region you sell in, and decide deliberately where to follow the market and where to stand apart.'),
],
'couture-menswear-guide': [
 ('h2', 'Other pieces in the wardrobe'),
 ('ul', ['<b>Jodhpuri suit:</b> a bandhgala jacket with matching trousers, formal and close to Western suiting.',
         '<b>Indo-western jacket:</b> shorter or asymmetric jackets worn over kurtas for sangeet and receptions.',
         '<b>Kurta and waistcoat (Nehru jacket):</b> the most worn occasion look, from mehendi to festivals.',
         '<b>Shawl and stole:</b> pashmina, jamawar or embroidered stoles add warmth and ceremony in winter weddings.']),
 ('h2', 'Fabrics and their uses'),
 ('ul', ['<b>Raw silk and dupion:</b> crisp, holds the sherwani line, takes embroidery well.',
         '<b>Brocade and jamawar:</b> woven pattern for the groom or close family; minimal embroidery needed.',
         '<b>Velvet:</b> rich and warm for winter receptions; best in deep colours.',
         '<b>Linen-silk and cotton-silk:</b> lighter options for day functions and warm climates.',
         '<b>Suiting wool:</b> for Jodhpuri suits and bandhgalas worn at formal dinners.']),
 ('h2', 'Fit points to check at each fitting'),
 ('p', 'At the first fitting, check the shoulder line, chest ease and sleeve pitch. At the second, check the collar stand, front edge and length, and the drape of the hem when the client sits. At the final fitting, check buttons and loops, embroidery alignment across seams and how the garment moves with a stole or safa.'),
 ('h2', 'Menswear occasion wear around the world'),
 ('p', 'Indian occasion menswear is worn far beyond India: at South Asian weddings in the UK, Canada, the US, the Gulf and Australia, and increasingly as eveningwear by non-South Asian clients. For these markets, lighter constructions, versatile colours such as ivory, navy and bottle green, and pieces that can be re-worn (a bandhgala with trousers, a kurta with a waistcoat) sell better than single-use outfits.'),
 ('h2', 'Pricing and lead time'),
 ('p', 'A bespoke sherwani with hand embroidery typically needs several weeks to a few months, depending on embroidery area and fittings. Communicate lead times early in the wedding season, and offer made-to-measure options with machine embroidery for shorter deadlines.'),
],
'couture-kids-occasion': [
 ('h2', 'Construction details that make a difference'),
 ('ul', ['<b>Full linings:</b> line embroidered panels completely so no stitches touch the skin.',
         '<b>Bound seams:</b> finish inside seams with soft binding instead of overlocking where possible.',
         '<b>Lightweight volume:</b> use soft tulle or cotton cancan rather than stiff nets for skirts.',
         '<b>Easy dressing:</b> side or back openings with zips or snaps; avoid long rows of hooks for young children.',
         '<b>Detachable layers:</b> jackets, capes or dupattas that can come off when it gets hot.']),
 ('h2', 'Fabric choices'),
 ('p', 'Cotton silk, chanderi, mulmul, soft georgette and lightweight raw silk work well. Avoid heavy brocades for very young children. For winter functions, use velvet sparingly as a jacket or waistcoat over a breathable base layer.'),
 ('h2', 'Safety checklist before production'),
 ('ul', ['Pull-test every bead, sequin, mirror and button.', 'Keep cords and ties short and away from the neck for younger age groups.', 'Check that dyes and finishes are suitable for children\'s skin and wash-tested.', 'Avoid small detachable parts on garments for children under three.', 'Label care instructions clearly; parents will wash these garments more than adult occasion wear.']),
 ('p', 'Many markets regulate children\'s clothing, for example rules on cords and drawstrings, small parts and chemical content. If you export, check the rules for each destination before finalising designs.'),
 ('h2', 'Where the demand is'),
 ('p', 'Coordinated family looks for weddings and festivals drive the category in India and in South Asian communities abroad. In the Gulf, Eid outfits for children are a major seasonal purchase. In Europe and North America, flower-girl and page-boy outfits and holiday party dresses follow the same rules: comfort, safety and a link to the adult outfits.'),
 ('h2', 'Ranging tip'),
 ('p', 'Build a children\'s range from three or four adult hero styles, simplified, and offer them in age bands. Matching colour and one motif are enough to make the family story clear.'),
],
'couture-innovations': [
 ('h2', 'Comfort engineering'),
 ('p', 'Long wedding days have changed what clients ask for. Ateliers are adding breathable linings, flexible boning, stretch side panels hidden under embroidery and detachable trains and capes so a single outfit can move from ceremony to dance floor. These changes rarely show in photographs, but they are now a key part of the sales conversation.'),
 ('h2', 'Better fit with fewer fittings'),
 ('p', 'Body scanning and digital pattern adjustment help ateliers with clients in other cities or countries. Measurements taken remotely can produce a closer first toile, so fewer in-person fittings are needed. The final fit is still checked by hand, but travel and time costs fall.'),
 ('h2', 'Material innovation in couture'),
 ('ul', ['Recycled and bio-based sequins and beads as alternatives to new plastic.', 'Metal threads with lower-impact plating for zari and zardozi.', 'Lighter organza and tissue weaves that keep shine with less weight.', 'Plant-based and recycled linings for structured pieces.']),
 ('p', 'These materials are still being tested at couture level. Ask suppliers for documentation and test samples for colour, weight and durability before using them on client pieces.'),
 ('h2', 'Service innovations'),
 ('ul', ['<b>Re-styling:</b> turning a bridal lehenga into separates or a gown after the wedding.', '<b>Heirloom rework:</b> integrating a parent\'s saree or embroidery into a new piece.', '<b>Rental for guests:</b> giving occasion pieces several lives.', '<b>Aftercare:</b> cleaning, storage and repair services that keep clients connected to the house.']),
 ('h2', 'What to watch next'),
 ('p', 'Forecut expects the most practical innovations, lighter structure and comfort engineering, to spread fastest, because they solve a real client problem. Digital tools will grow in made-to-measure and bridal. Novel materials will appear first in capsules and statement pieces. Follow the innovation radar on the fabrics page for timing.'),
],
'couture-history': [
 ('h2', 'Key moments in the twentieth century'),
 ('ul', ['<b>Early 1900s:</b> designers such as Paul Poiret moved fashion away from the corset towards looser, draped lines.',
         '<b>1920s–30s:</b> Gabrielle Chanel and Madeleine Vionnet introduced relaxed jersey dressing and bias cutting; Elsa Schiaparelli brought art into fashion.',
         '<b>1947:</b> Christian Dior\'s first collection, nicknamed the "New Look", brought back a cinched waist and full skirt after wartime restrictions.',
         '<b>1950s:</b> Cristóbal Balenciaga\'s sculptural, architectural shapes influenced a generation of designers.',
         '<b>1960s:</b> Yves Saint Laurent and others brought couture ideas into ready-to-wear boutiques.']),
 ('h2', 'Why history matters for designers'),
 ('p', 'Many "new" trends are reinterpretations. The defined waist and full skirts that Forecut tracks for SS27 echo the post-war New Look; the soft, draped tailoring rising for later seasons recalls the relaxed lines of the 1920s and 30s. Knowing the original helps designers update an idea rather than copy a past version of it.'),
 ('h2', 'Couture outside Paris'),
 ('p', 'Italy developed its own alta moda tradition in Rome and Florence after the Second World War. Couture tailoring has a long history in London\'s Savile Row. In India, royal courts supported textile and embroidery workshops for centuries, and techniques such as zardozi and jamdani have histories much older than Paris couture. Modern Indian couture brings these crafts into contemporary bridal and occasion wear.'),
 ('h2', 'Lessons that still apply'),
 ('ul', ['The silhouette defines an era: proportion changes are often the strongest signal of a new season.', 'Craft builds value: houses that invested in specialist workshops created lasting brands.', 'Couture leads, ready-to-wear follows: ideas move from the atelier to stores, often simplified.', 'Clients drive couture: direct relationships with clients shape every house\'s identity.']),
],
'couture-capitals': [
 ('h2', 'What each centre means for your brand'),
 ('ul', ['<b>Paris:</b> the reference for technique and innovation. Watch it for silhouettes and new craft.',
         '<b>Italy:</b> fabric and finishing. Watch for tailoring, eveningwear construction and textile quality.',
         '<b>London:</b> tailoring and experimental design. Watch for bespoke menswear and sculptural ideas.',
         '<b>New York:</b> red carpet and bridal. Watch for event dressing and how couture ideas are made commercial.',
         '<b>Beirut and Dubai:</b> embellished eveningwear for covered and modest dressing, and a strong Gulf client base.',
         '<b>India:</b> bridal craft at scale and some of the deepest embroidery skills in the world.']),
 ('h2', 'Craft networks behind each capital'),
 ('p', 'A couture capital is not only its designers. Paris depends on specialist ateliers for embroidery, feathers, pleating and millinery. Italy has mills and finishing workshops for silk, wool and jacquard. India\'s embroidery clusters, including those around Delhi, Lucknow, Mumbai, Kolkata and parts of Gujarat and Rajasthan, supply both Indian couture and international luxury brands. Understanding where the craft sits helps brands source well.'),
 ('h2', 'Emerging centres'),
 ('p', 'Couture and made-to-measure eveningwear are growing in other cities too, including Istanbul, Seoul, Shanghai, Lagos and São Paulo, where local designers combine regional craft with international silhouettes for a growing occasion-wear clientele. Saudi Arabia\'s fashion sector is also developing quickly, with a focus on local designers and eveningwear.'),
 ('h2', 'How to use this map'),
 ('p', 'If you design occasion wear, follow two or three centres closely rather than all of them: the one that sets the direction for your category, the one that matches your clients\' taste, and the one that supplies your craft. Forecut\'s region pages give the calendar and market notes for each.'),
],
'couture-gown-anatomy': [
 ('h2', 'Fabric layers from outside in'),
 ('ul', ['<b>Fashion fabric:</b> the visible silk, lace, organza or velvet.', '<b>Underlining:</b> a fabric such as silk organza basted to the fashion fabric to give body and hold embroidery.', '<b>Interlining or structure:</b> canvas, boning channels and padding where shape is needed.', '<b>Lining:</b> a smooth, breathable layer against the body, often silk.']),
 ('h2', 'Boning and support'),
 ('p', 'Spiral steel boning bends with the body in two directions and is often used in couture bodices; flat steel is used where a straight line is needed, such as the centre back. Lighter synthetic boning suits smaller or softer structures. Bones are placed along seams and darts to shape the torso and keep the bodice from collapsing.'),
 ('h2', 'Volume'),
 ('p', 'Skirts get their shape from layers of tulle, organza or crinoline, sometimes with horsehair braid in the hem to hold it out. The amount of volume is planned with the client\'s height and the event: a ballroom gown and a gown for a seated dinner are built differently.'),
 ('h2', 'Closures'),
 ('p', 'Couture gowns often close with hand-sewn hooks and bars, covered buttons and loops, or a concealed zip finished by hand. An inner corset may close separately, so the outer gown can be adjusted without pressure on the fabric.'),
 ('h2', 'What ready-to-wear can borrow'),
 ('ul', ['A waist stay in evening dresses to keep them in place.', 'Underlining under embroidered panels to prevent puckering.', 'Horsehair braid in hems for movement.', 'A well-finished interior: it is visible when the customer tries the piece on and influences the purchase.']),
 ('h2', 'Couture across cultures'),
 ('p', 'The same principles apply to a structured lehenga, a Gulf evening kaftan with embroidered yokes, or a Western ball gown: a fitted inner structure carries the weight, and decoration is planned on the pattern pieces before assembly.'),
],
'couture-bespoke-menswear': [
 ('h2', 'The bespoke process'),
 ('ul', ['<b>Consultation:</b> choosing cloth, style and purpose.', '<b>Measuring:</b> often more than 20 measurements, plus notes on posture and shoulder slope.', '<b>Pattern:</b> a unique paper pattern drafted for the client.', '<b>Baste fitting:</b> the garment is loosely tacked together and fitted.', '<b>Forward fitting:</b> closer to finished, with sleeves and collar set.', '<b>Finishing:</b> hand-sewn buttonholes, pressing and final checks.']),
 ('h2', 'Canvas construction'),
 ('p', 'A fully canvassed jacket has a layer of wool or horsehair canvas between the outer cloth and the lining, shaped and hand-stitched so the chest has natural curve and the lapel rolls softly. Half-canvas jackets use canvas only in the upper chest and lapel. Fused jackets glue an interlining in place; they are cheaper and quicker but less shapely and can bubble over time.'),
 ('h2', 'Cloth choices by climate'),
 ('ul', ['<b>Hot and humid climates (South and Southeast Asia, Gulf, West Africa):</b> open-weave wool, wool-linen, cotton and silk blends; half lining or unlined.', '<b>Temperate climates (Europe, North America):</b> worsted wool in mid weights year-round, flannel and tweed for winter.', '<b>Cold climates:</b> heavier flannels, cashmere blends and full canvas for warmth and shape.']),
 ('h2', 'Bespoke in Indian occasion wear'),
 ('p', 'The bandhgala and sherwani benefit most from bespoke methods: a canvassed front keeps the long line smooth, and the stand collar must sit precisely on the neck. Embroidery is planned after the pattern is final, so motifs sit on the collar, placket and cuffs in proportion to the client.'),
 ('h2', 'Offering made-to-measure well'),
 ('p', 'For brands that cannot offer full bespoke, a well-run made-to-measure service with clear measurement guidance, a fitting garment and one alteration covers most customers. It is also a strong way to sell occasion wear online to clients in other countries.'),
],
'couture-bridal-east-west': [
 ('h2', 'Silhouettes'),
 ('ul', ['<b>Western:</b> ball gown, A-line, mermaid, column and sheath, often with a train.', '<b>Indian:</b> lehenga with blouse and dupatta, saree, anarkali and sharara; grooms in sherwani or bandhgala.', '<b>Middle Eastern:</b> structured gowns with long sleeves and high necklines, often with capes and heavy embellishment.', '<b>East Asian:</b> many brides wear a white gown and a traditional outfit, such as the red qipao or hanbok, on the same day.', '<b>West African:</b> coordinated outfits in aso-oke, lace and wax prints, with headwraps (gele) and matching outfits for guests (aso ebi).']),
 ('h2', 'Colour traditions'),
 ('p', 'White became the standard Western bridal colour in the nineteenth century. Red is traditional in much of India, China and Vietnam, linked with luck and celebration. Today, colour choices are more personal everywhere: pastel lehengas in India, coloured gowns in Europe and the US, and gold and champagne for evening receptions in the Gulf and Africa.'),
 ('h2', 'Multi-event weddings'),
 ('p', 'Many weddings now include several events, each with its own outfit. South Asian weddings may include mehendi, haldi, sangeet, ceremony and reception. Western weddings often add a rehearsal dinner and an after-party look. This creates demand for lighter second looks, convertible pieces and coordinated family outfits.'),
 ('h2', 'Design ideas from the meeting point'),
 ('ul', ['A corset-structured lehenga blouse with a detachable cape.', 'A white or ivory gown with Indian hand embroidery on the bodice and train.', 'A reception gown in a lehenga fabric, such as embroidered organza or brocade.', 'Bridal veils with borders of gota, zardozi or lace, used across cultures.']),
 ('h2', 'Planning a bridal range'),
 ('p', 'Plan by event, not only by look: the main ceremony outfit, one or two lighter outfits and a reception piece. Give each a clear role in the range, a price point and a lead time. Forecut\'s couture themes and region pages show which directions are rising in each market.'),
],
}
